Route rule keys can now be scoped to a named request condition with `METHOD:NAME /path`. Conditions match request headers and are defined in `routeRules()` options.

```ts
import { routeRules } from "h3/rules";

app.use(
  routeRules(
    {
      // GET /blog/hello with `Accept: text/markdown` → 307 /blog/hello.md
      "GET:MD /blog/**": { redirect: "/blog/**.md" },
      // Already Markdown: don't redirect again
      "GET:MD /blog/*.md": { redirect: false },
    },
    {
      conditions: {
        MD: { headers: { accept: /\btext\/markdown\b/ } },
      },
    },
  ),
);
```

- A request that satisfies a condition keeps every plain `GET` rule and gets the condition's rules on top. When several conditions are satisfied, all of them apply.
- Header checks: `true` means present, `false` means absent, a string must match exactly, and a `RegExp` is tested against the value.
- Rule sets without conditions do no extra work per request.
- `cache` entries and memoized matches are kept separate per condition.
- The compiler supports conditions: `compileRouteRules(config, { conditions })` exports `resolveRouteRulesMethod(event, method)` for the compiled matcher.

Also fixes a `preMerge` bug where a method-scoped rule on a shared route node (`GET /a/:id`) hid an agnostic rule (`/a/*`) for `GET /a`.

